  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nShowers have continued to generate into the predawn hours thanks\nto upper divergence associated with a crossing shortwave trough,\nplus precipitable water values lingering in the 1.8 to 2.0 inch\nrange. As a result, the Flood Watch was extended for a portion\nof southwest Pennsylvania through 09Z. These factors will start\nto tail off after that time as the shortwave departs, with \ndecreasing support for lift as well as slowly dropping moisture \nlevels. Thus, only scattered showers should be left by that \npoint, with perhaps only a few ridge showers remaining by 12Z. \nLow clouds and fog will be prevalent through the morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAfter ceilings lift/mix out by early afternoon, we will be\nmonitoring the next severe threat, although there continues to\nbe uncertainty regarding the risk level. As a shortwave trough \ndigs into the Great Lakes, a surface cold front will drop across\nthe Upper Ohio Valley during the late afternoon/evening hours. \nDeep shear will be increasing into the 35 to 45 knot range ahead\nof the front, with even higher values behind it. There remains \nsome spread in the guidance regarding SBCAPE values ahead of the\nfront, although 25th/75th percentile values are higher than in \nprevious runs, with 25th percentile values from 900-1300 J/kg \nand 75th percentile levels at 1500-2000 J/kg. HREF member \nsoundings continue to suggest a warm layer at 800mb of various \nstrengths. The eventual instability levels will have \nimplications for convective coverage, updraft strength, and of \ncourse level of severe threat. For now, think that the Marginal \nRisk level sent by SPC is the way to go. However, if higher-end \ninstability levels seem more likely later on, a higher threat \nlevel may be warranted, especially as supercells may become more\nlikely. Damaging wind gusts and large hail remain the main \nimpacts. Any risk will end by around midnight as the front\nexits, although a few scattered showers may remain in its wake.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \nMinor timing issues remain, but ensembles have clustered into some \nbetter agreement that the trough axis crosses the region early \nMonday and maintains showers through the early morning hours before \nmid-level ridging provides a dry interlude by midday. This \nbreak may last until Tuesday night or Wednesday, depending on \nthe progress of the next front.",
